## 1 · THE CRITERION MODEL

⭐ **This section ships as-is. Do not rewrite it** — it is what lets a checker, a gate, or a person
with thirty seconds read every discipline the same way.

| Field | States |
|---|---|
| **ID** | ⭐ permanent — `{{XX}}-<area>-<nnn>` · ⛔ never renumbered |
| **Criterion** | what must be true |
| **Severity** | 🔴 mandatory · 🟠 expert · 🟢 guidance |
| **Verify** | ⭐ **how it is checked** — the observation, not the opinion |

### The three severities

| | Means | On violation |
|---|---|---|
| 🔴 **mandatory** | ⭐ security, money, or silent data loss | ⛔ **blocks** — never a matter of taste |
| 🟠 **expert** | the criterion of whoever owns this system | ⬜ its shipping effect is declared by the owner |
| 🟢 **guidance** | a preference with a reason | informs |

> ## ⛔ AN ID IS AN ADDRESS, NOT A POSITION
> ⭐ Something will cite `{{XX}}-AAA-003` from another file, a commit, or a review. **Renumbering
> breaks every one of those silently** — the citation still resolves, to the wrong criterion.
> **Deprecate an ID; never reuse it.**

⬜ **Pick your two-letter prefix** and check it against the seven that exist —
`BE` `DB` `FE` `PL` `DS` `VF` `VI`. ⛔ **A collision makes two criteria share one address.**

